  "dom_id": "step-q109745",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(8-\\lambda )^{6}(4+\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda -1)^{5},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8,$ there are exactly $4$ eigenvectors. If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8?$",
  "worked_solution": "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )=(8-\\lambda )^{6}(4+\\lambda )^{3}(\\lambda -1)^{5},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =8$ has multiplicity $6.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ is $6.$\n\nHowever, there are only $4$ eigenvectors corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =8$ is $4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =8$ is $6-4=2.$",
